<!DOCTYPE html>
{#
  Layout for app.tzortzoglou.eu.

  This does NOT reuse _includes/base.njk, on purpose. That layout is three quarters SEO
  contract - canonical, hreflang, Open Graph, Twitter, JSON-LD, the indexable collection -
  and every page here is behind a login and must never be indexed at all. Parameterising
  base.njk to switch off most of itself would couple two things that are diverging.

  What genuinely must not drift is shared instead of copied: the stylesheet, the fonts and
  the cache-busting and CSP transforms all come from eleventy.common.js.

  Front matter:
    title        the <title>
    bodyClass    classes on <body>
    redirectWhenAuthed  send an already-signed-in visitor to the app home
    shell        render the app's section sidebar beneath the shared bar
    navItems     [{label, href, navKey, minRole?}] for the shared bar's middle slot
    crossLink    {label, href} - the way back to the public site
    inlineScript end-of-body script
    pageStyle    inline <style> for this page
    bodyScripts  [{src, type, integrity, defer}] loaded at end of body
#}
{# `app-unknown` ONLY on the pages that carry the signed-in shell. Putting it on every app page
   hid the sign-in form, the register form, the reset form, the action page and the sign-out
   confirmation - none of which load shell.js, so nothing would ever have taken it off again. #}
<html lang="en" class="scroll-smooth dark{% if shell %} app-unknown{% endif %}">

<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="referrer" content="strict-origin-when-cross-origin">
  {# Must stay before the stylesheet, or the wrong theme paints first. Byte-identical to the
     public site's copy so both pages hash to the same CSP value. #}
  {% include "chrome/theme-boot.njk" %}
  {# Is there any reason to believe someone is signed in here?

     `app-unknown` on <html> hides the app's own chrome (see src/input.css). This takes it off
     when this browser has a profile hint, which is written only after /me has answered and
     cleared on every path that ends a session - so a returning signed-in visitor sees the rail
     with the page, and someone who has signed out, or never signed in, sees none of it while
     Firebase works out that they have to go to /login/.

     Before this, app.tzortzoglou.eu/ painted the whole signed-in home view - rail, section
     links, a Sign out button, the weather card - for about 40ms to anyone at all, then
     redirected. It looked like being signed in and then thrown out. #}
  <script>(function(){var d=document.documentElement;try{if(localStorage.getItem("app-profile")){d.classList.remove("app-unknown");return;}}catch(e){}
  /* Safety valve. shell.js takes the class off when /me answers, and Firebase comes from
     gstatic - so if that request is blocked, shell.js never runs and nothing would ever reveal
     the page. A visible shell that does not work can at least be diagnosed; a blank one looks
     like the site is broken. Long enough that the redirect to /login/ always wins the race. */
  setTimeout(function(){d.classList.remove("app-unknown");},6000);})();</script>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/poppins-400-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/poppins-700-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  {# Nothing here is for the public. The app is also excluded in robots.txt, but a header
     that only applies to pages nobody can reach is not much of a defence - this is. #}
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
  <title>{{ title }}</title>

  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/favicon-32x32.png">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0f172a" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#cbd5e1" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">

  <link href="/dist/output.css" rel="stylesheet">
  {%- if pageStyle %}
  {# Inline rather than a stylesheet: it is one page's worth of CSS, and the CSP transform
     hashes it automatically, so there is nothing to keep in sync. #}
  <style>
{{ pageStyle | safe }}
  </style>
  {%- endif %}
</head>

<body{% if redirectWhenAuthed %} data-redirect-when-authed="1"{% endif %} class="{{ bodyClass | default('bg-brand-bg text-brand-text antialiased min-h-screen flex flex-col') }}">

  <a href="#main-content" class="skip-link">Skip to content</a>

  {# The bar is the same file the public site uses, so the two properties cannot drift apart.
     What differs is only which links it is given. #}
  {% include "chrome/header.njk" %}

  {%- if shell %}
  {# The sidebar carries position *within* the app; the bar above carries identity and the way
     across to the public site. It stays hidden until /me answers, so the nav never offers a
     link the viewer cannot use. #}
  <div class="lg:flex">
    {# Not hidden, and not waiting for anything. It used to carry `hidden` until paint() ran,
       which is after the Firebase SDK loads from gstatic, after onAuthStateChanged fires, and
       after /me returns - so on every single navigation the rail vanished and came back, and the
       page beside it jumped sideways twice. The rail is part of the shell; only the two
       role-gated items are unknown at first paint, and the script below usually knows those too. #}
    {# Full height and anchored: `sticky` under the bar with its own scroll, so moving between
       sections never moves it. Home at the top because it is where you start; Private and Admin
       at the bottom because they are destinations you go to deliberately, not defaults; the
       profile below them because it is about you rather than about the app. #}
    <aside id="appHeader" class="hidden lg:flex lg:w-60 lg:shrink-0 lg:flex-col lg:border-r border-brand-muted/15 bg-brand-surface">
      <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-[77px] lg:h-[calc(100vh-77px)] flex flex-col">
        <nav aria-label="Application" class="flex flex-1 flex-col min-h-0 p-3">
          <ul class="space-y-1">
            <li>
              <a href="/" data-nav="home" class="side-link">
                {% icon "fa-solid-house", "side-icon" %}<span>Home</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          </ul>

          {# Pushed to the bottom rather than spaced apart, so the gap grows with the viewport
             instead of the items drifting down it. #}
          <ul class="mt-auto space-y-1 pt-6">
            <li class="hidden" data-min-role="SuperUser">
              <a href="/private/" data-nav="private" class="side-link">
                {% icon "fa-solid-heart-lock", "side-icon" %}<span>Private</span>
              </a>
            </li>
            {# Admin is not one page any more, so it is not one link any more. A <details> rather
               than a scripted menu: the browser already knows how to open and close a disclosure,
               announce its state and move focus through it, and this needs no JavaScript at all -
               which matters here, because the rail is drawn before Firebase has even loaded.

               It is open when you are already inside Admin, decided at build time from the URL,
               so arriving at a section never shows the tree closed over the page you are on and
               then opening. The sections themselves come from app/pages/_data/adminSections.js. #}
            <li class="hidden" data-min-role="Admin">
              <details class="group"{% if "/admin/" in page.url %} open{% endif %}>
                <summary class="side-link cursor-pointer select-none list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
                  {%- if "/admin/" in page.url %} data-section="current"{% endif %}>
                  {% icon "fa-solid-users", "side-icon" %}
                  <span class="flex-1">Admin</span>
                  {% icon "fa-solid-chevron-down", "h-3 w-3 shrink-0 text-brand-muted transition-transform duration-150 group-open:rotate-180" %}
                </summary>
                <ul class="mt-1 ml-4 space-y-1 border-l border-brand-muted/15 pl-2">
                  {%- for s in adminSections %}
                  <li>
                    <a href="{{ s.href }}" data-nav="{{ s.navKey }}" class="side-link">
                      {% icon s.icon, "side-icon" %}<span>{{ s.label }}</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                  {%- endfor %}
                </ul>
              </details>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        {# You. The preferences that used to sit here moved to /profile/ - a sidebar is
           navigation, and a control that changes your account is not navigation.

           `data-profile="loading"` until shell.js has heard back from /me. It shows a skeleton
           at exactly the height of the real thing and hides the real thing rather than removing
           it, so nothing in the rail moves when the answer lands. The three fields used to be
           empty elements: the block was blank for about a second, then grew as it filled. #}
        <div class="border-t border-brand-muted/15 p-3">
          <a href="/profile/" data-nav="profile" class="side-link" data-profile="loading">
            {% icon "fa-solid-user-gear", "side-icon" %}
            <span class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <span class="profile-real block">
                <span id="whoamiName" class="block truncate font-semibold"></span>
                <span id="whoami" class="block truncate text-xs text-brand-muted"></span>
                {# The role on its own line, not appended to the address. It used to be
                   "email · Role" on one truncated line, which made the role the first thing to
                   be clipped - the one part of that line short enough to always fit. #}
                <span id="whoamiRole"
                  class="mt-1 hidden w-fit rounded-full bg-brand-accent/10 px-2 py-0.5 text-[11px] font-semibold text-brand-accent"></span>
              </span>
              {# In the flow, not positioned over it. Absolute bars contribute no height, and the
                 three real fields are empty until /me answers - so the block measured 32px while
                 loading and 73px once filled, and the rail jumped by the 41px difference. That
                 was the whole bug; a skeleton that does not reserve the space is decoration.

                 The margins add up to the real block: 3+14 + 5+12 + 5+18 = 57px of content,
                 which with the link's py-2 is the 73px the filled version measures.
                 aria-hidden because a screen reader should hear the name when there is one,
                 not "loading" first and the name a second later. #}
              <span class="skel mt-[3px] h-[14px] w-24" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="skel mt-[5px] h-[12px] w-36" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="skel mt-[5px] h-[18px] w-14 rounded-full" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>
    {# Paints the role-gated items from the last known profile, before any network call. Without
       it, Private and Admin pop in a few hundred milliseconds into every page.

       It decides only what is DRAWN. The role here came from this browser's own storage and is
       worth exactly nothing as a claim: every byte that matters is refused by the API, which
       re-reads role and approval from Firestore on each request. Anyone can put "Admin" in their
       own localStorage and reach a page that tells them no.

       ORDER is an array and the lookup is indexOf, not an object literal. `RANK['constructor']`
       being a function rather than undefined is what let a role of "constructor" clear every
       gate on the server once; that shape does not get to exist again, even somewhere it could
       only mis-draw a link. #}
    <script>(function(){try{var p=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("app-profile")||"null");if(!p||typeof p.role!=="string")return;var ORDER=["User","SuperUser","Admin"];var have=ORDER.indexOf(p.role)+1;if(!have)return;document.querySelectorAll("[data-min-role]").forEach(function(i){var n=ORDER.indexOf(i.getAttribute("data-min-role"))+1;if(n&&have>=n)i.classList.remove("hidden");});}catch(e){}})();</script>
  {%- endif %}

  {%- if shell %}
    <main id="main-content" class="flex-grow min-w-0" aria-busy="true">
      {{ content | safe }}
    </main>
  </div>
  {%- else %}
  <main id="main-content" class="flex-grow">
    {{ content | safe }}
  </main>
  {%- endif %}

  {%- if shell %}
  {# A way to reach me from inside the app. Bottom right, out of the way of everything, and only
     on the pages a signed-in person actually uses - the auth pages have their own job. #}
  {# `items-end` is load-bearing. Without it the wrapper is as wide as the widest child, which
     is the panel, and the button - being narrower - sat at the left of that box: opening the
     panel shifted the button 269px to the left, away from the pointer that had just pressed it.
     A control must not move because of what it opened. #}
  <div class="fixed bottom-4 right-4 z-40 flex flex-col items-end print:hidden">
    <div id="cwPanel" class="hidden mb-3 w-[min(26rem,calc(100vw-2rem))] rounded-xl border border-brand-muted/15 bg-brand-surface p-5 shadow-xl"
      role="dialog" aria-modal="false" aria-labelledby="cw-title">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4">
        <h2 id="cw-title" class="text-base font-bold text-brand-text">Send me a message</h2>
        <button type="button" id="cwClose" class="icon-btn -mr-2 -mt-2 text-lg" title="Close">
          {% icon "fa-solid-times", "" %}<span class="sr-only">Close</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <p class="hint mt-1">
        From <span id="cwName" class="font-semibold text-brand-text"></span>
        (<span id="cwEmail"></span>) — I reply to that address.
      </p>

      <form id="cwForm" method="post" novalidate class="mt-4">
        <label for="cwMessage" class="field-label">Message</label>
        <textarea id="cwMessage" name="message" rows="4" maxlength="5000" class="field"></textarea>

        <label class="mt-3 flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-brand-muted">
          <input type="checkbox" id="cwConsent" name="consent" class="mt-1 shrink-0">
          <span>I am happy for this message and my address to be emailed to Stefanos and stored.</span>
        </label>

        <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-3">
          <button type="submit" class="btn-primary text-sm">Send</button>
          <p id="cwStatus" class="text-sm text-brand-muted" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>

    <button type="button" id="cwOpen" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="cwPanel"
      class="nav-cta bg-brand-surface shadow-lg">
      {% icon "fa-solid-envelope", "text-sm" %}<span>Message me</span>
    </button>
  </div>
  {%- endif %}

  {% include "chrome/footer.njk" %}

  {# Presentation only, and imports nothing - see scripts/chrome.js for why that matters. #}
  <script src="/scripts/chrome.js"></script>
  {%- for s in bodyScripts %}
  {# Literal src="..." with double quotes - the cachebust transform only versions that shape. #}
  <script{% if s.type %} type="{{ s.type }}"{% endif %} src="{{ s.src }}"{% if s.integrity %} integrity="{{ s.integrity }}" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"{% endif %}{% if s.defer %} defer{% endif %}></script>
  {%- endfor %}
  {%- if inlineScript %}
  <script>
{{ inlineScript | safe }}
  </script>
  {%- endif %}
</body>

</html>
